  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper ridge axis passes over the Great Lakes today, shunting \nsurface high pressure off to the south and east. Despite increasing \ncoverage of high cloud this afternoon, 1000-850mb thickness \nadvection will result in an uptick in high temperatures into the \nupper 80s and lower 90s. Can't rule out a stray late day shower in \nthe Saginaw Valley and Thumb where a weak surface trough may be able \nto capitalize on some weak instability; otherwise dry conditions \nprevail with little organized ascent expected.\n\nThe upper air pattern amplifies this weekend in response to a \nPacific jet streak arriving over the PNW. A shortwave meandering \nover the upper Midwest today reaches the western Great Lakes \nSaturday then interacts with a trough deepening overhead on Sunday. \nThis process sets off multiple rounds of ascent that pass overhead \nas a surface low tracks across northern/central IL into the Ohio \nValley. As deep layer moisture wraps around the north side of the \nlow, a wet pattern is in store for the weekend. Scattered showers \nand a few thunderstorms are forecast to arrive late tonight and \nSaturday morning as the initial arc of upper height falls and PVA \npivot through.\n\nScattered showers will remain in the vicinity through the day \nSaturday as the low begins to work into the lower Ohio Valley and \ndeeper moisture spreads in. A complete washout is not expected as \nseveral solutions still hold onto some low-level dry air, especially \naround Metro Detroit, which may limit coverage during the day. A few \nthunderstorms are also possible but in general the near moist \nadiabatic lapse rates will limit instability. Increasing coverage of \nshowers seems reasonable by Saturday evening and overnight into \nSunday as the upper forcing begins to consolidate overhead, favoring \nan increasing fgen and deformation response. The environment will \nsupport moderate to heavy downpours as PWAT rises to around 1.75\" \n(90th percentile) with warm cloud depth on the order of 12 kft noted \nin soundings.\n\nThere remains a high amount of uncertainty regarding QPF magnitude \nand placement during this period owing to the scale of the forcing \nas well as uncertainty in the track/intensity of the low. In \ngeneral, the ensemble solution space indicates high probability for \nat least 0.25\" of rainfall with reasonable potential for up to \n1.50\". Will note that the last several runs of the NAM are high \noutliers within the grand ensemble envelope, but do indicate the low \nprobability high end outcome for storm totals over 3\" in the event a \ndeeper low drives a targeted corridor of training convection along a \nnearly stationary fgen axis. WPC has highlighted all of SE MI within \na marginal risk for excessive rainfall Saturday and Sunday.\n\nThe system slowly migrates east by Monday as an upper ridge quickly \nadvances into the Great Lakes. There is increasing potential for the \nupper circulation to become orphaned over the Ohio Valley early in \nthe week, which could keep some showers in the vicinity of Metro \nDetroit and south. High pressure building in from the north should \notherwise maintain relatively benign and seasonable conditions into \nthe midweek period.",
